= Selection bias =

== Overview ==
'''Selection bias''' is systematic distortion caused by how units, participants, observations, or data enter, remain in, or are excluded from a sample or analysis.

Selection bias is a methodological/statistical problem. It is not a cognitive bias merely because both use the word ''bias''.

== Sampling ==
A sample can misrepresent a target population when inclusion probability is related to variables relevant to the outcome.

Examples include volunteer samples, nonresponse, loss to follow-up, and convenience sampling.

== Conditioning and colliders ==
In causal inference, selection can create associations that were not present in the target population.

If selection depends on a variable influenced by two causes, conditioning on that selection variable can induce collider bias.

== Survivorship ==
Analyzing only units that survive a filtering process can hide failures.

This can make successful cases appear more representative than they are.

== Selection bias and random error ==
A small sample can produce random sampling variability without systematic selection bias.

Conversely, a very large biased sample can estimate the wrong target very precisely.

Therefore:

<pre>
selection bias ≠ sampling variability
</pre>

== Selection bias and confirmation bias ==
[[Confirmation bias]] concerns cognitive search or interpretation favoring a belief.

Selection bias concerns which observations are present in the dataset or analysis.

A research process can contain both, but they are different mechanisms.

== Mitigation ==
Strategies include probability sampling, careful eligibility definitions, tracking attrition, weighting, sensitivity analysis, causal diagrams, and explicit specification of the target population.

No statistical correction can recover information that is absent without assumptions.

